[size=6][b]Devotion - Blessings and Penalties (Cross-Race Overview)[/b][/size]

This is the short companion to the race guides. Each race guide carries its own
per-tier blessing tables and its own list of what its gods dislike - you do not
need this page to play your race. What lives here is the handful of rules that
work the same for everyone, plus the one thing worth seeing all in one place:
which races get a scripted Champion special power, and which do not.

All values in the race guides are current beta numbers and may be tuned before
release. Where a guide and the game ever disagree, trust the in-game spell
description.

[size=5][b]How the Tiers Work[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Focused (patron) ladder:[/b] Seeker at 25 piety, Devoted at 50, Champion at 85. Only the highest tier you have reached is active - the tiers do not stack.
[*][b]Broad worship[/b] (honoring several gods at once, or a whole sect or pantheon) tops out at the Faithful tier, roughly equivalent to Devoted. Reaching Champion always means letting one single god clearly lead.
[*][b]One family at a time.[/b] While a focused god's gifts are active, your always-on cultural blessing steps aside, and only that one god's ladder is live.
[*][b]One save per race.[/b] At most one of your Champion-tier gifts is a scripted "cheat-death" power (see below). Every other Champion tier is a strong passive; its "special" is the recognition itself - your patron naming you - not an active proc.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Champion Special Powers[/b][/size]

Most Champion tiers are passive stats. A few carry a scripted power on top - almost
always a once-a-day near-death save. Each one below is verified against the shipped
records; the rest of a race's Champion tiers are passive.

[list]
[*][b]Khajiit[/b] [i]Baan Dar[/i] - Baan Dar Remembers - Once per day, when a blow would drop you (health falls to a fifth or lower), Baan Dar slips you out of death's hand - you are put back on your feet instead of dying. Khajiit's single cheat-death.
[*][b]Nord[/b] [i]Shor[/i] - Shor Remembers - Once per day, a killing blow becomes a last stand - Shor pulls you back from the edge and you survive it. Nord's single cheat-death.
[*][b]Redguard[/b] [i]HoonDing[/i] - HoonDing Makes Way - Once per day, a lethal blow is refused - HoonDing opens the road back from the edge and you keep your feet. Redguard's single cheat-death.
[*][b]Redguard[/b] [i]Tu'whacca[/i] - Death-rite restoration - From Devoted upward, keeping a death-rite - putting the restless dead to rest, or carrying a Far Shores token - restores a flat measure of health once a day: about 30 at Devoted, 50 at Champion. A flat restore, so it is felt even under Requiem. A heal, not a cheat-death, so it does not count as the race's save.
[*][b]Dunmer[/b] [i]The Ancestors[/i] - The Ancestors Watch - Armed by keeping the home prayer (pray at your ancestral urn or hearth). For that day, if you are brought to the brink, once the ancestors pull you back. Disarmed at dawn, so it is re-earned daily. Dunmer's single cheat-death.
[*][b]Bosmer[/b] [i]Baan Dar (Bandit Road)[/i] - Baan Dar Opens the Gap - On the Bandit Road, once a day a killing blow becomes an opening - you slip it and break away. Bosmer's single cheat-death.
[*][b]Argonian[/b] [i]Sithis (the Void)[/i] - Void near-death surge - While the Void is your active focus, once a day near death the Nothing lends a brief surge - a burst of stamina and momentum to fight your way clear.
[*][b]Orc[/b] [i]Malacath (any focus)[/i] - The Code Holds - With Malacath's regard, once in a fight when near death the Code holds - a flat burst of health (more at Devoted and up) keeps you standing.
[/list]

Altmer, Breton, and Imperial have no scripted power: every one of their Champion
tiers is a strong passive, and the reward is the recognition itself.

A cheat-death fires at most once a day (Orc's holds once per fight) and only when
your health has already dropped to a fifth or less; it will not save you from a
single overwhelming hit that skips that window.

[size=5][b]Dislikes and Disfavor[/b][/size]

Acting against a god's values loses piety with that god - raising the undead, killing
the defenseless, feeding as a vampire, and the rest. Offenses come in small, medium,
and large weights, and like everything else they are anti-farm capped, so a single
grave act costs more than a hundred petty ones. A real offense can also bring a short
"disfavor" - a mild, temporary debuff in the god's domain that fades on its own.

The specific acts each of your gods dislikes, and which god minds them most, are
listed in your race guide under "How You Lose Piety."

[size=5][b]Neglect and Curses[/b][/size]

Neglect is the absence of attention, not a punishment. Drift away from a god for a
few days and the small graces stop and a gentle debuff sets in; it lifts the moment
you take the work up again. A god you have committed to is shielded from time-based
decay, and broad worship suppresses per-deity neglect entirely. Two races are
different: the Argonian Hist and the Dunmer ancestors only bite when a curse has
silenced them, and the orthodox Bosmer Old Contract can make abandonment permanent.

Curses - vampirism and lycanthropy - are spiritual conditions that strain or break
faith, and what they mean varies sharply by race. Your race guide's "If You Are
Cursed" section, and the How Devotion Works primer, cover both in full.
